# Licensing

COMPITUM is **dual-licensed**: you may use it under either of the following, at your option.

| Licence | Text |
|---|---|
| Apache License 2.0 | [`LICENSES/Apache-2.0.txt`](LICENSES/Apache-2.0.txt) |
| MIT License | [`LICENSES/MIT.txt`](LICENSES/MIT.txt) |

`SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0 OR MIT`

Copyright 2026 The COMPITUM Project and contributors.

---

## What this means in practice

**Yes, you may use COMPITUM commercially, for free, without asking.** That is what a permissive
licence means, and it is deliberate. There is no "Pro" tier of the CLI, no feature gate, no
licence key, and no seat count. See [`docs/LICENSING.md`](docs/LICENSING.md) §4 for the list of
things this project commits to never charging for.

The only thing the code licence does **not** grant is the project **name**; see
[`TRADEMARK.md`](TRADEMARK.md).

---

## Not only code: the compatibility data has its own licence

This project produces two datasets, and they are licensed differently because they are created
differently. The distinction is what makes the project financially sustainable, and it is worth
understanding before you build on the data.

| Dataset | Licence | Where |
|---|---|---|
| Community-contributed observations | `ODbL-1.0` | `oracle-community/` |
| Observations produced by this project | Proprietary | `oracle-verified/` |

**`ODbL-1.0` permits commercial use.** It does not prevent you from using community data in a
business, and it does not restrict any field of endeavour. What it requires is that if you
**build and publicly use a derivative database**, that database stays under the same terms — so
nobody can take the community data, improve it, and enclose the improvement.

Creating a **report, chart, API answer or assessment** from the data is explicitly *not* a
derivative database, and no obligation attaches to it. See
[`docs/LICENSING.md`](docs/LICENSING.md) §5 for the precise clause-by-clause reading, including
the one requirement that is easy to violate by accident.

